Otitis Externa
Paediatric & Neonatal
Tragus/pinna tenderness, Edematous ear canal, Purulent otorrhea

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-035
Reason for Visit: Exquisite, severe localized ear pain and discharge requiring urgent evaluation to rule out mastoiditis or acute tympanic membrane rupture.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and mother
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented an older child crying in severe pain when the outer ear is touched, accompanied by weeping discharge. Immediate assessment was required to definitively distinguish acute external canal infection from deep middle ear/mastoid space infection and initiate targeted topical antimicrobial therapy prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "If anyone even brushes against my ear it hurts terribly, and it feels completely blocked and itchy inside."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with exquisite localized pain immediately elicited directly upon applying mechanical traction to the pinna. The symptoms developed over the last 48 hours. The patient reports intense, highly distressing pruritus strictly localized deep within the affected right external ear canal, accompanied by an uncomfortable pressure sensation and aural fullness. There is scanty, frankly purulent otorrhea directly weeping from the canal. The patient notes a mild conductive hearing loss on the right. There is a clear, highly specific history explicitly involving frequent daily swimming in a local lake over the past week.
Functional Status: Visibly uncomfortable, refusing to lie on the right side.
Social/Safety Concerns: Disrupted sleep due to severe pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 | HR: 90 | Temp: 37.1C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 35 Kg
General: Alert, protecting the right side of the head, afebrile, in distinct pain upon examination.
Physical Exam: Otoscopic examination reveals an erythematous, severely edematous, and physically narrowed right external auditory canal prominently lacking distinct middle ear involvement. Traction on the tragus and pinna elicits severe, exquisite localized pain. Purulent, foul-smelling debris is visible within the canal. The tympanic membrane is completely normal, structurally mobile, and distinctly pearly grey exclusively when explicitly visible past the edema. There is distinctly tender, palpably enlarged preauricular reactive lymphadenopathy directly draining the affected side. The mastoid process is completely non-tender to deep palpation. The diagnostic tetrad of tragus/pinna tenderness, edematous ear canal, and purulent otorrhea is definitively confirmed.
Environmental Observations: Swimming gear visible in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral ibuprofen taken 4 hours ago with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Otitis Externa (Swimmer's Ear) - Status: Acute, localized infection
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out explicitly by the direct visualization of a normal, non-bulging tympanic membrane and the presence of severe pain specifically strictly elicited by traction on the outer pinna.

Mastoiditis - Ruled out definitively by the absence of post-auricular erythema, absence of downward displacement of the pinna, and absolute lack of tenderness over the mastoid bone.

Ear Canal Foreign Body - Ruled out by direct otoscopic visualization revealing diffuse inflammatory edema rather than an impacted distinct object, corroborated by the clear swimming history.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk: Low, requires topical intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of severe otalgia and purulent discharge necessitated an urgent after-hours assessment to safely exclude deep space infections. Telephone triage accurately recognized the intense pain characteristic of acute ear pathology. Objective clinical evaluation confirmed a severe, localized infection of the external auditory canal while explicitly ruling out acute otitis media and the surgical emergency of mastoiditis. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the patient to continued, unbearable pain and risked complete edematous occlusion of the canal, which would subsequently prevent the necessary administration of topical therapies. Immediate intervention provided diagnostic clarity and allowed for the initiation of targeted topical antibiotic/corticosteroid drops.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Ciprofloxacin/Dexamethasone otic drops, 4 drops BD for 7 days. Advised strict continuation of oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs for analgesia.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe pain of administering drops into an inflamed canal, instruct the patient to physically hold the medication bottle in their closed fist for exactly 5 minutes prior to instillation. By actively warming the drops to body temperature, the patient directly eliminates the painful caloric reflex (vertigo and stinging) caused by cold liquid hitting an inflamed eardrum, strongly reinforcing independent compliance with the medication schedule.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3-5 days to ensure the canal edema is resolving.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the redness and severe swelling explicitly spread outward down the neck, if the bone behind the ear becomes red and agonizingly tender, or if the patient develops a high fever.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The ear canal swelling may take 48-72 hours to significantly recede despite appropriate topical treatment.
Clinical Course: The pain will remain intense for the next two days. The drops must be continued for the full 7 days even after the pain stops to completely eradicate the bacteria.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if the bone directly behind your ear becomes swollen, red, or very painful to touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The skin lining the outside tube of the ear is infected, usually by water-loving bacteria from swimming. The eardrum itself is completely safe. The ear drops contain an antibiotic to kill the bacteria and a strong steroid to shrink the severe swelling. Oral antibiotics do not work well for this specific outer ear infection.
Actionable Steps: Keep the ear strictly dry. Do not swim. When showering, place a cotton ball heavily coated in petroleum jelly into the outer bowl of the ear to block water entry. Absolutely never insert cotton swabs into the canal.

SMS INFORMATION

